{"bill":{"id":"119-sjres-31","congress":119,"billType":"sjres","billNumber":"31","title":"A joint resolution providing for congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the Environmental Protection Agency relating to \"Review of Final Rule Reclassification of Major Sources as Area Sources Under Section 112 of the Clean Air Act\".","introducedDate":"2025-03-06","latestActionDate":"2025-06-20","latestActionText":"Became Public Law No: 119-20.","policyArea":"Environmental Protection","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Air quality","Congressional oversight","Environmental Protection Agency (EPA)","Legislative rules and procedure"],"sponsorBioguideId":"C001114","cosponsorBioguideIds":["C001047","C001096","L000571","S001198","H001061"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"This joint resolution removes the requirements for sources of persistent and bioaccumulative hazardous air pollutants to comply with certain major source emissions standards when those sources reclassify as area sources (which have less stringent standards). Specifically, the joint resolution nullifies the Environmental Protection Agency rule titled Review of Final Rule Reclassification of Major Sources as Area Sources Under Section 112 of the Clean Air Act . The rule requires sources that seek to reclassify as area sources to implement certain control methods (e.g., continue to employ emission control methods required under the major sources requirements) to ensure they do not increase emissions beyond the major source requirements applicable at the time of reclassification.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-joint-resolution/31","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/sjres/BILLSTATUS-119sjres31.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:23.489Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-sjres-31","plainSummary":"This joint resolution would nullify an EPA rule that requires industrial facilities to maintain strict emission controls when they reduce their pollution output enough to reclassify from \"major source\" to \"area source\" status under the Clean Air Act. The EPA rule was designed to prevent facilities from increasing emissions of persistent and bioaccumulative hazardous air pollutants after reclassifying to the less stringent area source category. If this resolution passes, facilities that reclassify would no longer be required to continue using the emission control methods that were mandatory when they were classified as major sources.","industries":[{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"Industrial manufacturers are frequently classified as major sources of hazardous air pollutants and would face changed regulatory obligations regarding emission controls after reclassification."},{"industry":"energy-fossil","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"Fossil fuel facilities often emit hazardous air pollutants and would be directly affected by changes to emission control requirements when reclassifying source status."},{"industry":"mining","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Mining and materials processing operations can be major sources of hazardous air pollutants subject to these reclassification rules."},{"industry":"waste-water","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"Waste management facilities may emit hazardous air pollutants and could be affected by changes to emission control requirements during source reclassification."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"e16a321323f7224d01080e28","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:44:19.653Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This joint resolution would remove the requirements for sources of persistent and bioaccumulative hazardous air pollutants to comply with certain major source emissions standards when those sources reclassify as area sources (which have less stringent standards).","whoItTouches":"People living near a road, a plant or a port.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in the air where you live. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is further down this page.","titleInPlainWords":null},"overlaps":[{"billId":"119-sjres-31","bioguideId":"S001198","cycle":2026,"score":0.01172677081142237,"matches":[{"industry":"energy-fossil","donorAmount":104400,"donorShare":0.024367256341510775,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.006790874718125953},{"industry":"manufacturing","donorAmount":47500,"donorShare":0.011086634829710363,"billConfidence":0.9,"contribution":0.003271466015324369},{"industry":"mining","donorAmount":22500,"donorShare":0.005251563866704908,"billConfidence":0.7,"contribution":0.0012052769530142412},{"industry":"waste-water","donorAmount":10000,"donorShare":0.0023340283852021815,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.0004591531249578061}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.6968112695591695},"member":{"name":"Dan Sullivan","chamber":"Senate","state":"AK","imageUrl":"data/portraits/S001198.jpg","role":"Cosponsor"},"donorProfile":{"bioguideId":"S001198","cycle":2026,"totalItemized":4284438.04,"byIndustry":[{"industry":"finance-banking","amount":171083.33000000002,"share":0.0399313348454912,"contributionCount":28},{"industry":"energy-fossil","amount":104400,"share":0.024367256341510775,"contributionCount":28},{"industry":"legal","amount":95510,"share":0.022292305107066035,"contributionCount":27},{"industry":"transport","amount":93000,"share":0.02170646398238029,"contributionCount":34},{"industry":"party-leadership","a